    <h2 id="pms">Saffron for PMS</h2>
    <p>In a double-blind trial of 50 women with PMS, 15 mg of saffron twice daily for two menstrual cycles cut total PMS symptoms and depression scores significantly more than placebo (Agha-Hosseini 2008). Irritability, mood swings and low mood improved most. Read our full guide to <a href="/saffron-for-pms/">saffron for PMS</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="menopause">Saffron for Menopause and Hot Flashes</h2>
    <p>A randomized trial gave post-menopausal women with depression and hot flashes 30 mg/day of saffron (15 mg twice daily) or placebo for 6 weeks. The saffron group had greater reductions in both hot-flash scores and depression scores (Kashani 2018). It's a single small study, but it's one of the few herbal options with randomized evidence for both symptoms at once.</p>
    <p class="note">Hot flashes have many effective treatments, including hormone therapy and non-hormonal medicines. Saffron is a reasonable thing to discuss with your doctor, not a replacement for a menopause consultation.</p>

    <h2 id="postpartum">Saffron for Postpartum Mood</h2>
    <p>In mothers with mild-to-moderate postpartum depression, 15 mg of saffron twice daily for 8 weeks improved depression scores more than placebo (Tabeshpour 2017). Safety data for saffron supplements during breastfeeding are still limited, so this is only something to try with your doctor's involvement.</p>
    <div class="note note-warn"><strong>Postpartum depression is common and treatable.</strong> If you feel persistently low, anxious or detached after having a baby, please talk to your doctor or midwife. In the US you can call or text the National Maternal Mental Health Hotline at 1-833-852-6262, or 988 in a crisis.</div>

    <h2 id="libido">Saffron for Libido and Sexual Function</h2>
    <p>Antidepressants often reduce libido and arousal. In a 4-week trial of women with fluoxetine-related sexual dysfunction, 15 mg of saffron twice daily improved arousal, lubrication and pain scores compared with placebo (Kashani 2013). A 2019 meta-analysis of saffron and sexual dysfunction found benefits across men and women, though the trials were small (Ranjbar 2019).</p>

    <h2 id="mood">Everyday Mood and Stress</h2>
    <p>Most saffron mood trials included both women and men. In healthy adults with subclinical low mood, 28 mg/day of affron® improved mood and reduced tension within 4 weeks (Kell 2017). See <a href="/saffron-for-depression-and-mood/">saffron for mood</a> for the full evidence.</p>

    <h2 id="pregnancy">Pregnancy and Breastfeeding</h2>
    <div class="flag flag-red"><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> large amounts of saffron have traditionally been used to stimulate the uterus. A pinch in food is considered fine, but avoid saffron supplements unless your obstetrician specifically approves them.</div>
    <div class="flag flag-amber"><strong>Trying to conceive:</strong> stop saffron supplements once you're actively trying, unless your doctor advises otherwise.</div>
    <div class="flag flag-amber"><strong>Breastfeeding:</strong> safety data are limited. Talk to your doctor first.</div>
    <p>Run through the <a href="/tools/saffron-safety-checker/">saffron safety checker</a> before starting.</p>

    <h2 id="how">How Women Can Take Saffron</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table>
